  "usage_note": "**Maybe** usually comes at the start of a clause. It is a little less formal than **perhaps**, which is more common in careful writing. **May be**, written as two words, is **may** followed by the verb **be**: *She may be late* means about the same as *Maybe she'll be late*.",
  "learner_errors": [
    {
      "incorrect": "May be she is at home.",
      "correct": "Maybe she is at home.",
      "note": "As an adverb at the start of a sentence, it is one word: **maybe**."
    },
    {
      "incorrect": "It maybe true.",
      "correct": "It may be true.",
      "note": "After a subject, the verb is two words: **may be**."
    }
  ],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": null,
    "grammar": "\"maybe\" (one word) is an adverb, while \"may be\" (two words) is a verb form: *Maybe it is true* and *It may be true*. Translators should check which one the English text uses.",
